e Configuration and Reproducibility Reproducing the paper from the released artefacts. Dataset fingerprints. Seeds. License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.02444v1 [cs.CL] 01 Oct 2026 Counterexample Generation via Per-Theorem Symbolic Verifiers: When Imitation Hurts and Reinforcement Repairs Omar Farouk Zouak † † thanks: Corresponding author: omar.zouak@ensia.edu.dz Affiliation: National School of Artificial Intelligence, Algiers, Algeria Houssam Eddine Boukhalfa Affiliation: National School of Artificial Intelligence, Algiers, Algeria Soumaya Lakehal Affiliation: National School of Artificial Intelligence, Algiers, Algeria Shiv Katiyar Affiliation: University of Birmingham, United Kingdom Samia Nefti-Meziani Affiliation: University of Birmingham, United Kingdom Abstract Large language models often solve a theorem forward yet fail to disprove a closely related false one: a falsification gap that supervised fine-tuning does not close and can actively worsen. We frame counterexample generation as constrained witness emission against a deterministic per-theorem Python verifier, and release SymCE , a corpus of 4,707 false undergraduate-algebra and real-analysis conjectures, each paired with executable verifiers. The verifier also serves as the reward function, making SymCE a training environment. Training Qwen3-4B with SFT followed by GRPO under this oracle reveals an imitation trap : counterexample-only SFT collapses true-theorem recognition from 0.27 to 0.00, while RLVR with a sparse outcome-only reward repairs this and exceeds the base, to 0.66. The collapse replicates across four seeds and on Gemma-3-4B. Sparse and dense rewards yield statistically indistinguishable in-domain success yet diverge by 33 points on a held-out calibration probe, a dissociation we trace to the partial-credit term. Our 4B model outperforms every evaluated 7B open-weights math specialist, remains competitive with six frontier commercial APIs, and transfers under unchanged prompting to GSM8K, MATH-500 and MMLU-college-math. A human audit of 177 verifier decisions finds 97.7 % accuracy. Code, data, verifier modules and annotations: https://github.com/ce-rlvr/SymCE . 1 Introduction Large Language Models (LLMs) excel at forward mathematical derivation, but struggle significantly with inverse reasoning and generating counterexamples to false claims. Recent benchmarks demonstrate that even frontier models fail at falsification tasks they can easily forward-solve ( Sinha et al., 2025 ; Li et al., 2025 ) . Furthermore, standard imitation learning (SFT) often exacerbates this by inducing format-biases rather than true logical reasoning ( Shao et al., 2025 ) . The natural fix is to train counterexample generation directly, but two obstacles have so far prevented this from becoming a primary objective. First, large-scale counterexample data is scarce: the only widely cited counterexample benchmark, COUNTERMATH, contains 1,216 problems, and its evaluation is mediated by an LLM judge. Second, supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on counterexamples appears insufficient. Counterexample-only training data contains, by construction, only false statements, and we show below that imitation-only training transfers this distribution shift into the policy: a model trained purely to emit counterexamples loses the ability to recognise true statements, and confidently fabricates witnesses on theorems that are in fact true. We attack both obstacles together. We build SymCE , a deterministic, large-scale counterexample environment, by hypothesis-dropping over 6,748 undergraduate-level theorems and generating, for each (theorem_id, dropped_hypothesis) pair, an independent Python verifier module that returns one boolean per remaining hypothesis and one for the negated conclusion. The pipeline retains 4,707 records. Evaluation is fully symbolic: the policy emits a one line Python expression, and the verifier returns a boolean tuple. Because the verifier runs in tens of milliseconds, the same substrate serves as the reward function during reinforcement learning, so SymCE is a training environment and not only a benchmark. We use this environment to train Qwen3-4B ( Qwen Team, 2025 ) with SFT followed by GRPO under two reward shapes and to evaluate against five 7B open-weights math specialists, six commercial-API frontier models, and four external mathematics benchmarks. In doing so, we uncover a pathology : imitation-only training on counterexamples collapses the policy’s ability to recognise true theorems, and only verifier-grounded RL with an outcome-only reward repairs it. Partial-credit dense rewards yield equal in-domain success but do not restore calibration; a dissociation that, to our knowledge, has not been previously isolated. Contributions. We make four main contributions. (C1) The Imitation Trap Pathology. We isolate a calibration failure mode in counterexample-only supervised fine-tuning: Pass@1 on true theorems collapses from 0.27 to 0.00 under the training prompt style, because the policy learns the output form of a counterexample without the negated-conclusion constraint. The collapse reproduces in all four training seeds and on a second model family. (C2) Verifier-Grounded RLVR for Falsification. We demonstrate that Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR), guided by a deterministic symbolic execution interface, repairs this pathology. Furthermore, we show that the partial-credit reward term that is the natural dense shaping for this task structure preserves logical calibration markedly worse than an outcome-only reward, and we identify the mechanism. (C3) Strong Empirical Performance & Transfer. Our Qwen3-4B RLVR model achieves 0.49 ± \pm 0.02 Pass@1 on in-domain falsification across four seeds, outperforming all evaluated 7B open-weights math specialists and remaining competitive with frontier APIs, while demonstrating strong zero-shot transfer to standard forward-math tasks (MATH-500, GSM8K). (C4) SymCE : A Deterministic Falsification Environment. To enable this training, we introduce and release a programmatically verifiable corpus of 4,707 false algebraic conjectures with per-theorem executable verifiers, moving beyond the noisy LLM-as-a-judge paradigm. 2 Related Work Counterexample and falsification benchmarks. The closest comparable resource for counterexample reasoning is COUNTERMATH ( Li et al., 2025 ) , a 1,216-problem benchmark of university-level counterexample-driven proofs evaluated with an LLM-as-judge protocol; the largest frontier model reported in that work achieves an F1 of 60.1. REFUTE ( Sinha et al., 2025 ) measures counterexample success on algorithmic problems and reports a sharp asymmetry between forward solution rates (around 48 %) and counterexample success (below 9 %) for the same models. The most directly related training resource is Learning to Disprove ( Li et al., 2026 ) , which constructs roughly 575K hypothesis-dropping mutations of Lean 4 Mathlib theorems and uses Lean tactic checks as the verification signal. Our dataset is smaller than Li et al. ’s but uses a Python-verifier substrate that is more faithful to the algebraic and real-analytic structures evaluated, runs in tens of milliseconds rather than seconds, and is paired with the witness-schema interface of § 3.2 . The Alchemy line of AST-mutation work ( Wu et al., 2024 ) focuses on generating valid mutated true theorems for automated theorem proving and is complementary rather than competing. Verifiable-reward reinforcement learning for mathematics. RLVR with GRPO has become the dominant post-training paradigm for forward mathematical reasoning, anchored by DeepSeek-R1 ( Guo et al., 2025 ) and adopted in PRIME ( Cui et al., 2025 ) and Eurus ( Yuan et al., 2024 ) . While recent studies debate whether RLVR installs new reasoning or merely elicits latent capabilities via format adaptation ( Shao et al., 2025 ) , our out-of-distribution transfer results (§ 6.5 ) suggest falsification training installs fundamentally transferable inverse-reasoning skills. Process supervision and symbolic verifiers. Process reward models (PRMs), introduced for mathematical reasoning by Lightman et al. (2024) and extended by Math-Shepherd ( Wang et al., 2023 ) , and the FOVER pipeline ( Kamoi et al., 2026 ) , which mines step-level labels from Z3 and Isabelle traces, supply dense supervision at the step level for forward proving. Our per-theorem verifier sits between an outcome reward model and a PRM: it returns one boolean per remaining hypothesis and one for the negated conclusion, which gives the reward function (Eq. 1 ) a structured, audited signal without the human-annotation cost of a learned PRM. PRL ( Yao et al., 2026 ) and self-rewarding correction ( Xiong et al., 2025 ) bear directly on where such an intermediate signal sits; our boolean tuple differs from both in being externally grounded and non-learned, which is what lets § 6.3 attribute a behavioural change to one term of the aggregation. SymPy and Z3 ( Meurer et al., 2017 ; De Moura and Bjørner, 2008 ) provide the underlying verification primitives. The hallucination tax. A growing literature describes a calibration pathology induced by standard imitation-style training: TreeCut ( Ouyang, 2025 ) demonstrates that LLMs continue to fabricate answers when problems are systematically modified to remove a critical constraint; SUM ( Song et al., 2025 ) and related work report refusal-rate collapses of roughly 80 % under standard rejection-sampling fine-tuning on unanswerable-math benchmarks. Our imitation-trap finding (§ 6.2 ) is the cleanest measurement of this pathology in the counterexample-generation setting we are aware of, and the first to localise the mechanism, via the reason taxonomy of § 6.3 , to the partial-credit term of the reward function. 3 Dataset and Verification Environment We frame counterexample generation as a constrained generation task: given a false conjecture of the form “if H 1 ∧ ⋯ ∧ H k H_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge H_{k} then Q Q ”, output a witness w w such that all remaining hypotheses hold and Q Q fails on w w . Our environment is built around three design choices: a per-theorem Python verifier compiled once and reused across all rollouts, a structured witness-schema interface that constrains the policy’s output shape, and a conclusion-weighted dense reward whose sparse counterpart we use for ablation. 3.1 Source theorems and hypothesis dropping We collected 6,748 unique undergraduate-level mathematical theorems spanning six subdomains (Group, Ring, Field and Number Theory, Linear Algebra and Real Analysis). Each theorem is stored in a structured form with explicit hypotheses and conclusion fields. To construct false conjectures we drop one hypothesis at a time, giving 18,262 candidates. Removing a hypothesis does not guarantee the result is false or even well-posed — the hypothesis may be redundant or merely clarificatory — so we apply an LLM-driven filter (Gemini 3.1 Pro): the teacher must classify the mutated theorem as well-posed and non-trivial, emit a valid Python verifier, and propose a counterexample that evaluates to True on that verifier without execution errors, with up to 5 retries under error-trace feedback. The teacher produces a verifier for 12,376 candidates; gating then distils these to 4,707 verifier-backed records over 3,485 unique theorems, of which 4,700 carry a witness schema (§ 3.2 ). We report per-subdomain counts in Table 1 . A sandboxed subprocess imports the verifier dynamically, builds the witness-evaluation namespace, evaluates the policy’s witness expression string in that namespace, and calls conditions(witness) , which returns a list of booleans, one per remaining hypothesis followed by one for the negated conclusion. The policy and the oracle share no code paths beyond Python’s eval ; the verifier is fixed at the start of training and reused across all rollouts for that conjecture. This is a deliberate departure from prior counterexample-evaluation pipelines: COUNTERMATH ( Li et al., 2025 ) relies on an LLM-as-judge protocol, and Learning to Disprove ( Li et al., 2026 ) relies on a Lean tactic check. A Python module evaluated against a witness expression is intermediate: it is symbolic and decidable for the subdomains we target, fast (§ 3.5 ), and reproducible. 3.2 Witness-schema interface We attach to each prompt a one-line witness schema : a short Python-shape description of an acceptable witness. Schemas are generated post-hoc by reading the verifier module and its canonical witness with a second teacher model (DeepSeek) and asking for a single-sentence description of the expected Python object shape, with one or two concrete examples. Sample schemas are shown in Appendix H . Schemas are merged into the user prompt. We view this as a small but load-bearing methodological contribution: a structural prior on the output shape, generated automatically from the verifier and a canonical witness, is what allows for the practical RLVR effectiveness without altering either the reward function or the verifier. 3.3 Split construction We construct three splits with per-domain stratified sampling and a fixed seed (42): the held-out test set (n=201), the SFT split (n=1,000), and the RLVR prompt set (n=3,506). Table 1 summarises the splits. 3.4 Reward function The per-rollout reward computed by the oracle is r = \displaystyle r= α ⋅ s hyp n hyp + β ⋅ q ¬ + γ ⋅ 𝟏 ​ [ format ok ] \displaystyle\alpha\cdot rac{s_{	ext{hyp}}}{n_{	ext{hyp}}}+ eta\cdot q_{
